The problem (SIGSEGV) occurs when trying to apply the following regex to a large string when using GCC 13/14/15 (it does not occur with GCC 16): #include <regex> #include <string> #include <format> #include <iostream> int main() { std::regex regex; regex = "(^|
)?(\*+)(\s+)([^
]+)(
([\s\S]+))?"; std::string test = R"(* Headline Text )"; test.resize(64 * 1024); std::sregex_iterator sRegexBegin = std::sregex_iterator(test.begin(), test.end(), regex); std::sregex_iterator sRegexEnd = std::sregex_iterator(); std::size_t matchCount = std::distance(sRegexBegin, sRegexEnd); std::cout << std::format("match count: {}
", matchCount); for (std::sregex_iterator i = sRegexBegin; i != sRegexEnd; ++i) { std::cout << std::format("sub match count: {}
", i->size()); } } The problem is a SIGSEGV triggered at the line std::sregex_iterator sRegexBegin = std::sregex_iterator(test.begin(), test.end(), regex); . It does not occur when the regex is changed to "(^|
)?(\*+)(\s+)([^
]+)(
(.+))?" , but this ignores the 
 , which is not the intended behavior. The code is here . How can I solve this without changing the compiler? Obs.: This is a simplified part of a parser that uses regex, but I am almost giving up on using regex, as it seems that a raw scanner that parses the input character by character might be a better approach.
